#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""wiki-crosslink — find the edges a source-backed page owes but has not written.

Pure stdlib, no dependencies, per repo convention.

Subcommands:
  entities [filter]   The entity index: every wiki page's page-name, aliases and
                      infobox handles, i.e. every string that can be looked for
                      in a source. `--missing` is the pages with none;
                      `--audit` grades the ones that exist.
  coverage [prefix]   Which readable corpora actually name a page, and how
                      often. The triage step — run it before scanning, because
                      most page/corpus pairs are silent and finding that out
                      costs ~3s for the whole wiki against all three corpora.
                      `--silent` inverts it: the pages nothing names, with the
                      ones that cite a corpus anyway called out.
  handles [prefix]    Which pages join to a message corpus by counterparty,
                      and which cannot. The message corpus's own blind spot,
                      the same shape as `entities --missing`: 30 pages resolve
                      and reach 41,349 rows; 138 declare no handle at all.
  scan <page>         Unlinked entities in the corpora a page's `sources:` cite.
                      `--against <corpus>` scans a corpus the page does NOT
                      cite, filtered to rows that name the page itself.
                      `--conversation` scopes by COUNTERPARTY instead, which is
                      the question a message corpus can answer — see below.
                      `--queue [n] --against <c>` is the campaign queue, pages
                      that state a limit about their own evidence first.
                      `--low` includes single-token name matches (mostly noise).
                      `--all` over the twitter year pages.
  counts [prefix]     Pages whose stated `| Messages |` count disagrees with
                      the corpus their handle resolves into. Usually the page
                      counted one direction and called the other half an
                      "export artifact".
  reciprocal [page]   Outbound typed edges whose target does not link back. The
                      inverse-edge obligation of CONNECTIONS_SPEC.md, computed
                      rather than remembered.
  orphaned            Pages whose `sources:` name a corpus this tool can read
                      and which link nothing mined from it — the pages where a
                      scan has never been run.
  check               Gate: reports the reciprocal debt as a warning banner and
                      exits 0. Never blocks a commit; see below.

Why this exists
---------------
A page assembled from a corpus records what its writer found *interesting*.
That is not the same set as what the corpus *contains*, and the gap between
them is invisible from the page — which is exactly the condition CLAUDE.md's
"depth is the binding constraint" describes. Nineteen twitter year pages were
written by reading every tweet in each year, and they carry 130 typed edges
between them; the archive names people, places, works and concepts that have
their own wiki pages several times that often. Every one of those is a
connection nobody can make later, because synthesis reasons from `wiki/` and
the mention never reached it.

This tool does not write edges and must not be made to. It produces
CANDIDATES, and a candidate is a reason to go and read the source rows it
names. `skills/corpus/vocabulary-drift.md` is the standing warning about what a
string match over a long corpus measures: a count is a measurement of the
matcher's vocabulary, and it is worthless as a finding. It is not worthless as
a pointer, which is the only thing used here — every row this prints carries the
dated source lines that produced it, so the verification step is reading them,
never trusting the count.

Three properties of the matching, stated because they decide how a result is
read:

1.  **Confidence is a property of the string, not of the match.** An @handle or
    a two-token full name is unambiguous; a bare first name ("Bill", "Al",
    "Jess") matches any Bill. Single-token names are reported at `low` and are
    never to be acted on without reading the row.
2.  **A mention is not a relationship.** The archive naming a person is
    evidence the page may owe an edge, and nothing more. A tweet that mentions
    Diplo is not a Diplo relationship; it is a music-consumption datapoint that
    may belong on a different page entirely. The type and the claim come from
    reading, per CONNECTIONS_SPEC.md.
3.  **The right scope depends on the corpus, and `--against` is wrong for
    messages.** That mode reads the rows naming a page and asks what else they
    name. A tweet is a self-contained utterance that often names several
    handles, so it works. A message averages five to fifteen words and one that
    names somebody almost never names a second — run over the campaign queue
    against the 217,573-record dump it returned **0 candidates across 7 pages**,
    which is a property of the mode, not a finding about the pages. A message
    corpus knows something no broadcast archive does: **who the counterparty
    is.** `scan --conversation` scopes by that, joining a page's infobox
    `handles:` to the corpus's own handle field, and reaches 41,349 rows across
    30 people pages.
4.  **Absence here is not absence in the corpus.** The index can only look for
    names somebody already wrote a page for. Entities with no page are exactly
    the ones a scan cannot see, and finding them is a reading job.
5.  **A page name that is also ordinary English produces confident garbage,
    and this is catchable.** Confidence is "more than one token = high", which
    a two-token name that is a phrase people just say breaks in the worst
    direction. Measured over the message dump: `say anything` returns **134
    rows and none is the band**; `the office` returns **77 and none is the
    show** — every one is a real estate office. `coverage` was presenting the
    first as the wiki's top music result. Such candidates are now **flagged
    rather than demoted** (some really are the band), with the count marked as
    not-evidence. `mark_common_phrases()`.
6.  **A string two pages both claim is evidence for neither.** `@alexisarmel`
    is in one person's `aliases:` and in another's infobox `handles:`, so 85
    rows about the first were offered as 101 mentions of the second. Every such
    candidate is now marked `contested` with the competing name, because the
    index knows and the output used to not say.
7.  **The index is only as good as `aliases:`, and 389 of 497 pages have none.**
    Matching is exact-phrase on a page's title, and a title is almost never how
    a thing is written in a source. `wiki/interests/opie-and-anthony` is the
    worked case: the archive names it 61 times — "o&a" 8, "opie" 25, "cumia"
    10, "@OpieRadio" 18 — and the exact string "opie and anthony" appears
    **zero** times, so `coverage` reports the corpus silent on it. This is not
    a tuning problem, it is missing frontmatter, and populating `aliases:` is
    worth more to this campaign than any further work on the matcher.
    `entities --missing` is the list.

Why `check` never fails
-----------------------
The reciprocal debt is real work but it is not a corruption: a one-way edge
still carries its claim, and the target page is merely poorer for not having
it. A gate that went red on it would block every unrelated commit until 40
pages had been read, and CLAUDE.md is explicit about what happens to a
mandatory step that blocks unrelated work — it acquires an escape hatch and
stops being mandatory. `bin/wiki-connect check` already warns per-edge; this
prints the total so the trend is visible.
"""
import os, re, sys, json, bisect, collections

ROOT = os.path.dirname(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
WIKI = os.path.join(ROOT, "wiki")

# Corpora this tool knows how to read, keyed by the `sources:` path a page cites.
#
# `family` exists because the two message exports are NOT two forms of one
# corpus, which is what CROSSLINK_CAMPAIGN.md assumed before anybody measured
# it. Overlap on (timestamp, first 120 normalised chars), 2026-09-04:
#
#     dox-scan   215,057 unique rows        csv   174,775 unique rows
#     shared     124,379                    csv-only  50,396   dox-only  90,678
#
# So each holds a third of the record the other does not, and reading either
# alone loses it. They are deduped against each other when both are in play —
# `family` is what says they may be — and the dedupe is reported rather than
# silent, because a row count is the first thing a session reads off a scan.
CORPORA = {
    "raw/self/twitter/archive.jsonl": {
        "kind": "jsonl", "date": "created_at", "text": "text", "family": None,
        "note": "public broadcast; no counterparty, no inbound",
    },
    "raw/self/dox-scan/all_imessages_complete_dump.txt": {
        "kind": "doxdump", "family": "messages",
        "note": "direction reliable; 2015-2025 with 2012-2014 and 2022 empty, no 2026",
    },
    "raw/self/message-csv/MASTER_MESSAGES_DB_DUMP.csv": {
        "kind": "msgcsv", "family": "messages",
        "note": "the only message corpus covering 2026; 79% of Sent rows carry no handle",
    },
}

# ---- THE MORATORIUM IS ENFORCED HERE, NOT REMEMBERED -----------------------
#
# CLAUDE.md's standing directive stops the record on Annie advancing: no new
# narrative, event, synthesis or typed-edge claim about her. A tool whose whole
# job is to hand a session pages to write edges onto must not put hers in the
# queue, and must not answer a scan of one — a scan output *is* a worklist, and
# the pilot's queue surfaced her page at position five.
#
# Same shape as `bin/wiki-plain` in being a refusal rather than a warning, and
# deliberately NARROWER in what it refuses — see under_moratorium() for the
# measurement behind that. Only the operator changes it.
MORATORIUM = re.compile(r"\b(annie|ulmer)", re.I)


def under_moratorium(txt, path=None):
    """True for a page that IS about her — not for one that mentions her.

    **This threshold is deliberately narrower than `bin/wiki-plain`'s and the
    difference is not an oversight.** That tool forbids her name appearing in
    any file under `plain/` at all, so a page naming her three times cannot get
    a twin without either naming her or distorting the page; its INCIDENTAL=2
    body-mention rule follows from that and is correct there.

    Here the risk is different. The directive forbids writing new claims *about
    Annie*. Crosslinking `wiki/self/tattoos` to a Misfits tweet does not do
    that. Measured 2026-09-04: porting wiki-plain's rule across would refuse
    **197 of 497 pages** — including `interests/golf`, `self/tattoos` and
    `music/aliases/sloppp` — because she is a decade-long presence who gets
    mentioned in passing everywhere. That is 40% of the wiki withheld from the
    campaign for no safety gained, since none of those edges would be about her.

    So the refusal is scoped to her own pages, by title and aliases. The rest
    of the directive is a constraint on **what a session writes**, not on what
    it may look at, and `scan` prints the reminder on any page that names her.
    """
    fm, _ = split_fm(txt)
    # The name has to be resolved the way the rest of this file resolves it.
    # This read `scalar(fm, "title")` alone until 2026-09-04, and **288 of 497
    # pages carry no `title:` field at all** — their name lives in
    # `infobox.name` or in the slug, which `title_of()` already knows and the
    # guard did not. Nine pages were invisible to it in consequence, among them
    # `wiki/timeline/annie-record`, `wiki/people/ellen-ulmer` and
    # `wiki/timeline/events/shelbie-annie-threesome-april-2019` — pages squarely
    # about her, which `scan` would have accepted as subjects and offered as
    # targets. Same shape as the hole `bin/wiki-plain`'s guard shipped with: a
    # safety check whose failure mode is silent permission.
    #
    # `path` is optional so the existing call sites keep working; without it the
    # slug fallback is unavailable and only the frontmatter is read.
    head = " ".join([
        title_of(path or "", fm),
        scalar(fm, "title") or "",
        " ".join(inline_list(fm, "aliases")),
        os.path.basename(path)[:-3].replace("-", " ") if path else "",
    ])
    return bool(MORATORIUM.search(head))


# Pages that ARE about her, computed once at startup from the same test that
# refuses a scan of one. Held as a module global because render_hits needs it
# per candidate and load_pages() is not free.
MORATORIUM_TARGETS = set()


def note_moratorium_targets(pages):
    MORATORIUM_TARGETS.clear()
    for path, txt in pages.items():
        if under_moratorium(txt, path):
            MORATORIUM_TARGETS.add(path[:-3])
    return MORATORIUM_TARGETS


def mentions_moratorium(txt):
    _fm, body = split_fm(txt)
    return len(MORATORIUM.findall(body))


# Pages written by a generator, where a hand-added inverse edge is destroyed on
# the next run and fails that generator's own check. An edge INTO one of these
# is legitimate and is simply not reciprocable by hand: the tool reports them
# apart from the debt rather than counting work nobody may do. Keep in step
# with CLAUDE.md's list of generated surfaces.
GENERATED = {
    "wiki/meta/testimony-veracity",
    "wiki/meta/skills",
    "wiki/meta/readers-digest",
    "wiki/meta/digest",
    "wiki/meta/recent-activity",
    "wiki/meta/open-questions",
    "wiki/health/intake-ledger",
    # Generated by the timeline builder and 883KB of it. It was absent from
    # this set while `bin/wiki-lint` carried it, which is two lists of the same
    # fact disagreeing: `reciprocal` was asking for inverse edges on a page
    # where a hand-written edge is destroyed on the next regeneration.
    "wiki/timeline/master-timeline",
}


def is_generated_slug(slug):
    return slug in GENERATED

# Name tokens too common to carry a match on their own. A page whose entire
# name is one of these can only be found by reading.
STOPNAMES = {
    "dan", "frank", "mom", "dad", "mother", "father", "aunt", "uncle", "the",
    "and", "one", "two", "new", "old", "big", "red", "man", "boy", "girl",
    "annoying", "bub", "md", "al", "bill", "brian", "jess", "jimmy", "del",
    "felix", "clyde", "marla", "manuel", "bud", "chip", "buddy", "junior",
}

FM_LIST_RE = re.compile(r'^\s*-\s+(.+?)\s*$')


def split_fm(txt):
    if txt.startswith("---"):
        e = txt.find("\n---", 3)
        if e > 0:
            return txt[3:e], txt[e + 4:]
    return "", txt


def load_pages():
    pages = {}
    for root, _dirs, files in os.walk(WIKI):
        for f in sorted(files):
            if not f.endswith(".md"):
                continue
            p = os.path.relpath(os.path.join(root, f), ROOT)
            with open(os.path.join(root, f), encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
                pages[p] = fh.read()
    return pages


def scalar(fm, key):
    m = re.search(r'^%s:\s*(.+?)\s*$' % re.escape(key), fm, re.M)
    if not m:
        return None
    v = m.group(1).strip()
    if v.startswith(("'", '"')) and v.endswith(("'", '"')) and len(v) > 1:
        v = v[1:-1]
    return v or None


def inline_list(fm, key):
    """Read `key: ["a", "b"]` or an indented `- ` block, at any nesting."""
    out = []
    m = re.search(r'^\s*%s:\s*\[(.*?)\]\s*$' % re.escape(key), fm, re.M | re.S)
    if m:
        # Split on commas and strip quotes per item. An earlier alternation-based
        # regex left the quotes on every entry after the first — the third branch
        # matched ` "Lexie"` whole — which silently demoted every quoted @handle
        # to a low-confidence single token, because the `startswith("@")` test
        # was looking at `"@alexisarmel"`. It cost 83 of 84 matching rows on one
        # page before anybody noticed, so this stays boring on purpose.
        for part in m.group(1).split(","):
            v = part.strip().strip('"\'').strip()
            if v:
                out.append(v)
        return out
    m = re.search(r'^(\s*)%s:\s*$' % re.escape(key), fm, re.M)
    if m:
        indent = len(m.group(1))
        for line in fm[m.end():].splitlines():
            if not line.strip():
                continue
            cur = len(line) - len(line.lstrip())
            if cur <= indent:
                break
            lm = FM_LIST_RE.match(line)
            if lm:
                out.append(lm.group(1).strip().strip('"\''))
    return out


def edges(fm):
    """[(page, type), ...] from a `connections:` block."""
    out, cur = [], None
    for line in fm.splitlines():
        m = re.match(r'^\s*-\s+page:\s*(.+?)\s*$', line)
        if m:
            cur = [m.group(1).strip().strip('"\''), None]
            out.append(cur)
            continue
        m = re.match(r'^\s+type:\s*(.+?)\s*$', line)
        if m and cur is not None:
            cur[1] = m.group(1).strip().strip('"\'')
    return [(p, t) for p, t in out]


INVERSE = {
    "causes": "caused-by", "caused-by": "causes",
    "evidences": "evidenced-by", "evidenced-by": "evidences",
    "instantiates": "instance-of", "instance-of": "instantiates",
    "precedes": "follows", "follows": "precedes",
    "supplies": "supplied-by", "supplied-by": "supplies",
    "component-of": "contains", "contains": "component-of",
}
SYMMETRIC = {"contradicts", "parallels", "mirrors", "co-occurs"}
NO_INVERSE = {"escalates", "resolves", "contextualizes"}


def title_of(path, fm):
    t = scalar(fm, "title")
    if t:
        return t
    ib = re.search(r'^infobox:\s*$', fm, re.M)
    if ib:
        n = re.search(r'^\s+name:\s*(.+?)\s*$', fm[ib.end():], re.M)
        if n:
            return n.group(1).strip().strip('"\'')
    return os.path.basename(path)[:-3].replace("-", " ")


def build_index(pages):
    """slug -> {names: [(string, confidence)], domain, title}"""
    idx = {}
    for path, txt in sorted(pages.items()):
        fm, _ = split_fm(txt)
        slug = path[:-3]
        title = title_of(path, fm)
        names = collections.OrderedDict()

        def add(s, conf):
            s = (s or "").strip()
            if not s or len(s) < 3:
                return
            if s.lower() in STOPNAMES:
                return
            if s.lower() not in names or conf == "high":
                names[s.lower()] = (s, conf)

        # The page's own name, and the slug read as words.
        for cand in (title, os.path.basename(slug).replace("-", " ")):
            toks = [t for t in re.split(r'\s+', cand) if t]
            conf = "high" if len(toks) > 1 else "low"
            add(cand, conf)
        for a in inline_list(fm, "aliases"):
            toks = [t for t in re.split(r'\s+', a) if t]
            add(a, "high" if (a.startswith("@") or len(toks) > 1) else "low")
        for h in inline_list(fm, "handles"):
            if h.startswith("@"):
                add(h, "high")
        idx[slug] = {
            "names": list(names.values()),
            "domain": scalar(fm, "domain") or "?",
            "title": title,
            "limit": self_limit_score(txt),
        }
    return idx


def _mine_messages():
    """The message-dump reader, imported rather than rewritten.

    `bin/mine-messages` already carries the three properties that make naive
    reading of that dump wrong — multi-line records reassembled, curly quotes
    folded, direction trustworthy only in this file — and every one of them had
    already produced a false measurement before it was written. A second reader
    here would inherit none of that and would look identical from outside,
    which is the failure mode CLAUDE.md's Phase 2 note warns about in advance:
    "a reader written without them produces confident wrong candidates".
    """
    import importlib.machinery, importlib.util
    p = os.path.join(ROOT, "bin", "mine-messages")
    loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader("_mine_messages", p)
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_loader("_mine_messages", loader)
    mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    loader.exec_module(mod)
    return mod


_CORPUS_CACHE = {}


def load_corpus(rel):
    """[(timestamp, text, meta), ...] for one corpus. Memoised.

    Memoised because it stopped being cheap. The twitter archive is 1.3 MB and
    was re-read per page without anybody noticing; the message dump is 217,573
    records and re-reading it per page in a `--queue` run would have turned a
    scan into a coffee break.
    """
    if rel in _CORPUS_CACHE:
        return _CORPUS_CACHE[rel]
    spec = CORPORA[rel]
    kind = spec["kind"]
    path = os.path.join(ROOT, rel)
    rows = []
    if kind == "jsonl":
        datekey, textkey = spec["date"], spec["text"]
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
            for line in fh:
                line = line.strip()
                if not line:
                    continue
                try:
                    d = json.loads(line)
                except ValueError:
                    continue
                rows.append((d.get(datekey, ""), d.get(textkey, "") or "",
                             d.get("url", "")))
    elif kind == "doxdump":
        mm = _mine_messages()
        for r in mm.records(path):
            rows.append(("%s %s" % (r["date"], r["time"]),
                         mm.norm(r["text"]),
                         "%s %s" % (r["dir"], r["handle"] or "-")))
    elif kind == "msgcsv":
        # Direction is present and split nearly evenly here (88,988 Sent /
        # 86,370 Received, 2026-09-04) — but 69,869 of those Sent rows carry no
        # contact_handle at all, so this corpus can say WHAT Dan wrote and, four
        # times in five, not to whom. That is the trap to carry: it is sound for
        # a corpus-wide claim and unsound for a per-relationship one.
        mm = _mine_messages()
        import csv as _csv
        _csv.field_size_limit(10 ** 9)
        with open(path, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace", newline="") as fh:
            for r in _csv.DictReader(fh):
                txt = r.get("text") or ""
                if not txt.strip():
                    continue
                rows.append(((r.get("timestamp") or "").strip(),
                             mm.norm(txt),
                             "%s %s" % (r.get("direction") or "?",
                                        (r.get("contact_handle") or "-").strip() or "-")))
    _CORPUS_CACHE[rel] = rows
    return rows


def dedupe_key(ts, text):
    """Two exports of the same message must count once. See CORPORA above."""
    return (ts.strip(), re.sub(r"\s+", " ", text).strip().lower()[:120])


TOKEN_RE = re.compile(r"[a-z0-9@']+")

# Words too common to serve as the trigger token for a name. Only used to pick
# WHICH token of a multi-word name gets indexed — never to reject a match.
TRIGGER_STOP = set(
    "the a an and or of in on at to for is it my me you i we he she they them "
    "that this with his her from as by be was were are am not no yes do did"
    .split())


class Matcher:
    """Name lookup by inverted token index rather than one giant alternation.

    The alternation worked at 1.3 MB and did not survive the message corpus.
    Measured 2026-09-04 over the 217,573-record dump: a single `re.search` per
    row against the high band (a 19,418-character alternation) took **50.1s**.
    The same pass through a token index takes **0.6s** for the same 862 rows —
    80x, and the difference between a tool a session runs and one it works
    around.

    The index maps one trigger token per name to the names carrying it, so a
    row is only tested against names sharing a word with it. The trigger is the
    longest non-stopword token, as a cheap stand-in for the rarest. Every
    surviving candidate is then confirmed with a real word-boundary regex, so
    the index only decides what to *test* and never what matches.
    """

    def __init__(self, idx):
        self.owner = {}
        self.conf = {}
        self.trigger = collections.defaultdict(list)
        self._rx = {}
        for slug, meta in idx.items():
            for name, conf in meta["names"]:
                key = name.lower()
                self.owner.setdefault(key, set()).add(slug)
                if conf == "high" or key not in self.conf:
                    self.conf[key] = conf
        for key in self.conf:
            toks = [t for t in TOKEN_RE.findall(key) if t not in TRIGGER_STOP]
            if not toks:
                toks = TOKEN_RE.findall(key)
            if not toks:
                continue
            self.trigger[max(toks, key=len)].append(key)

    def _boundary(self, key):
        rx = self._rx.get(key)
        if rx is None:
            esc = re.escape(key)
            pat = (esc + r"\b") if key.startswith("@") else (r"\b" + esc + r"\b")
            rx = self._rx[key] = re.compile(pat)
        return rx

    def find(self, text):
        """[(name, conf), ...] — every indexed name this text contains."""
        low = text.lower()
        toks = set(TOKEN_RE.findall(low))
        out = []
        for t in toks & self.trigger.keys():
            for key in self.trigger[t]:
                if key in low and self._boundary(key).search(low):
                    out.append((key, self.conf[key]))
        return out

    def names_rx(self, names):
        """A boundary regex over an explicit name list — the own-name filter."""
        if not names:
            return None
        return re.compile("|".join(
            (re.escape(n.lower()) + r"\b") if n.startswith("@")
            else (r"\b" + re.escape(n.lower()) + r"\b") for n in names), re.I)


_INDEX_CACHE = {}


def corpus_index(rels, matcher):
    """One pass over the union of `rels`. Memoised per corpus set.

    Returns (rows, hits, slug_rows):
      rows      [(timestamp, text, meta, corpus_basename), ...] deduped
      hits      {row_index: [(name, conf, slug), ...]}
      slug_rows {slug: [row_index, ...]}

    Built as an index rather than re-scanned per page because both questions
    the campaign asks — "which corpora name this page" (coverage) and "inside
    the rows that name it, what else is named" (scan --against) — are lookups
    against the same pass. Before this, `coverage` over 497 pages ran the whole
    corpus once per page.
    """
    key = tuple(sorted(rels))
    if key in _INDEX_CACHE:
        return _INDEX_CACHE[key]
    families = {}
    for rel in key:
        families.setdefault(CORPORA[rel].get("family"), []).append(rel)
    rows, dropped = [], 0
    for fam, rels_in in sorted(families.items(), key=lambda kv: (kv[0] or "")):
        seen = set() if (fam and len(rels_in) > 1) else None
        for rel in rels_in:
            base = os.path.basename(rel)
            for ts, text, meta in load_corpus(rel):
                if seen is not None:
                    k = dedupe_key(ts, text)
                    if k in seen:
                        dropped += 1
                        continue
                    seen.add(k)
                rows.append((ts, text, meta, base))
    hits, slug_rows = {}, collections.defaultdict(list)
    for i, (_ts, text, _m, _b) in enumerate(rows):
        found = matcher.find(text)
        if not found:
            continue
        per = []
        for name, conf in found:
            for slug in matcher.owner.get(name, ()):
                per.append((name, conf, slug))
                slug_rows[slug].append(i)
        hits[i] = per
    out = (rows, hits, dict(slug_rows), dropped)
    _INDEX_CACHE[key] = out
    return out


def page_range(fm):
    return scalar(fm, "date_range_start"), scalar(fm, "date_range_end")


# Names that are also ordinary English. Populated by mark_common_phrases().
COMMON_PHRASE = set()
WORD_RE = re.compile(r"[a-z']+")
COMMON_TOP_N = 1000


def mark_common_phrases(idx, rels):
    """Flag every page name that is also a phrase people just say.

    **This is the one class of the campaign's "uncatchable" failure that is
    catchable, and it was producing confident garbage at HIGH confidence.**
    The confidence rule is `more than one token = high`, on the reasoning that a
    bare first name matches anybody and a full name does not. A two-token name
    that is also ordinary English breaks it, and breaks it in the worst
    direction — the tool tells you to trust the match.

    Measured 2026-09-04 over the 217,573-record dump:

      "say anything" (a band with a page)   134 rows, **0 of them the band**
      "the office"   (a show with a page)    77 rows, **0 of them the show** —
                                             every one is Suz's real-estate office

    `coverage` was presenting the first of those as the wiki's top music result.
    An edge written from it would have been correctly cited, plausibly worded
    and entirely false.

    **Flagged, not demoted, and the difference is deliberate.** Demoting hides
    the candidate behind `--low`, and some of these names really are the band:
    Fall Out Boy is discussed by name in the Ally conversation. Flagging leaves
    the candidate in front of the reader and removes the only dangerous thing
    about it, which is the count being quoted as evidence. A false flag costs a
    session one glance; a missing flag cost 134 phantom mentions.

    Discovery is self-calibrating rather than a hand-kept list: the ordinary
    words are the corpus's own top-N tokens, so the test tracks how the record
    is actually written. It over-flags, on purpose — "annie ulmer" trips it,
    because a surname belonging to the most-messaged person in the corpus is
    genuinely frequent in it. Dispersion was tried as a fix (how many separate
    conversations a token reaches) and abandoned: 504 handles, most of them a
    handful of messages, so even "say" reaches only 14.7% of them and nothing
    separates.
    """
    COMMON_PHRASE.clear()
    freq = collections.Counter()
    for rel in rels:
        for _ts, text, _m in load_corpus(rel):
            freq.update(WORD_RE.findall(text.lower()))
    if not freq:
        return COMMON_PHRASE
    common = {w for w, _n in freq.most_common(COMMON_TOP_N)}
    for _slug, meta in idx.items():
        for name, conf in meta["names"]:
            if conf != "high" or name.startswith("@"):
                continue
            toks = WORD_RE.findall(name.lower())
            if len(toks) > 1 and all(t in common for t in toks):
                COMMON_PHRASE.add(name.lower())
    return COMMON_PHRASE


def corpus_handles(rels):
    """{handle: row-count} over the named corpora. Memoised via load_corpus."""
    out = collections.Counter()
    for rel in rels:
        for _ts, _t, meta in load_corpus(rel):
            h = (meta or "").split(" ", 1)
            if len(h) == 2 and h[1] and h[1] != "-":
                out[h[1]] += 1
    return out


def resolve_handles(fm, known):
    """A page's declared handles, resolved against the ones a corpus actually has.

    The join a message corpus makes possible and a broadcast archive cannot: a
    page names a phone number or an address in its infobox, and the corpus knows
    which rows are the conversation with that counterparty. That is a far
    stronger scope than "rows that happen to name the page", which on a corpus
    of five-to-fifteen-word messages is nearly always empty — a message
    mentioning somebody rarely mentions a second person in the same breath.

    Handles on people pages are written four ways and all four are handled:
    full E.164, a bare ten digits, an email, and **partially masked**
    (`+1724***7658`), which is how this repository redacts a live number in a
    public file. A masked number is matched by pattern against the corpus's own
    handle list; measured 2026-09-04, every one of them resolved to exactly one
    handle, because a ten-digit space with three unknowns and a known area code
    is not ambiguous inside 504 contacts. A mask that matches two is reported
    as unresolved rather than guessed at.
    """
    bylast = {}
    for h in known:
        d = re.sub(r"\D", "", h)
        if len(d) >= 10:
            bylast.setdefault(d[-10:], []).append(h)
    emails = {h.lower(): h for h in known if "@" in h}
    hits, ambiguous = [], []
    for raw in inline_list(fm, "handles"):
        h = raw.split(" (")[0].strip()
        if not h or h.startswith("@"):
            continue                      # a social handle is not a counterparty
        if "@" in h:
            got = emails.get(h.lower())
            if got:
                hits.append(got)
            continue
        digits = re.sub(r"[^\d*]", "", h)
        tail = digits[-10:] if len(digits) >= 10 else digits
        if not tail:
            continue
        if "*" in tail:
            rx = re.compile("^" + tail.replace("*", r"\d") + "$")
            cand = [k for k in bylast if rx.match(k)]
            if len(cand) == 1:
                hits.append(bylast[cand[0]][0])
            elif len(cand) > 1:
                ambiguous.append((raw, len(cand)))
            continue
        if tail in bylast:
            hits.append(bylast[tail][0])
    return sorted(set(hits)), ambiguous


def scan_conversation(slug, pages, idx, matcher, corpora, limit=3):
    """Everything named inside the conversation with this page's counterparty.

    The source-mention obligation asked of a message corpus. Returns the same
    shape as scan_page so render_hits is unchanged.
    """
    txt = pages[slug + ".md"]
    fm, _ = split_fm(txt)
    known = corpus_handles(corpora)
    resolved, ambiguous = resolve_handles(fm, known)
    if not resolved:
        return ("NOHANDLE", ambiguous)
    linked = linked_targets(slug, fm, txt)
    rows, allhits, _slug_rows, _d = corpus_index(corpora, matcher)
    want = set(resolved)
    hits = collections.defaultdict(list)
    n_rows = 0
    for i, (ts, text, meta, base) in enumerate(rows):
        parts = (meta or "").split(" ", 1)
        if len(parts) != 2 or parts[1] not in want:
            continue
        n_rows += 1
        seen = set()
        for name, conf, tgt in allhits.get(i, ()):
            if tgt in linked or (name, tgt) in seen:
                continue
            seen.add((name, tgt))
            hits[tgt].append((ts[:10], name, conf, text, meta, base,
                              len(matcher.owner.get(name, ()))))
    hits["__rows__"] = n_rows
    hits["__handles__"] = resolved
    return hits


def cmd_entities(argv):
    pages = load_pages()
    idx = build_index(pages)
    if argv and argv[0] == "--missing":
        return cmd_entities_missing(idx, pages)
    if argv and argv[0] == "--audit":
        return cmd_entities_audit(idx, pages)
    filt = argv[0].lower() if argv else None
    n = 0
    for slug in sorted(idx):
        meta = idx[slug]
        if filt and filt not in slug.lower() and filt not in meta["title"].lower():
            continue
        names = ", ".join("%s[%s]" % (s, c[0]) for s, c in meta["names"])
        print("%-58s %s" % (slug, names))
        n += 1
    print("\n%d page(s) indexed" % n)
    return 0


SELF_LIMIT = re.compile(
    r"\b(complete record|the complete|only (?:source|evidence|record|trace)|"
    r"everything known|rests (?:entirely )?on|remains description|"
    r"no (?:ledger entries|corroboration|primary source|dated evidence|message corpus)|"
    r"blind window|testimony alone|his own telling|nothing else in the corpus|"
    r"not (?:datable|checkable|dated)|has never been checked|unmeasured)\b", re.I)


def self_limit_score(txt):
    """How loudly a page states a checkable limit about its own evidence.

    Measured 2026-09-04: 157 of 497 pages say something of this kind, and every
    finding of the pilot pass landed on one — four of them falsifying the claim
    outright. It is the difference between a queue and a pile, so it is computed
    rather than left to a session to notice.
    """
    return len(SELF_LIMIT.findall(txt))


WIKILINK_RE = re.compile(r'\[\[(wiki/[a-z0-9\-/]+)')


def linked_targets(slug, fm, txt):
    """What this page actually links — a typed edge, or a real `[[wikilink]]`.

    **This used to regex `wiki/[a-z0-9-/]+` over the whole page**, which counts
    every appearance of a path-shaped string as a link: a `sources:` entry, a
    typed edge's own `page:` field, a path named in prose, and — the one that
    did the damage — **a path in backticks**. `bin/build-site` linkifies `[[…]]`
    and nothing else, so a backticked path renders as inert grey code. The tool
    was reporting "nothing unlinked found" for pages that contained no link at
    all.

    Measured 2026-09-04, after the operator clicked a page and found none:
    **all 19 `wiki/self/twitter/` year pages carry zero body wikilinks between
    them, against 161 typed edges.** Two crosslink passes ran over that tree and
    both were told it was clean.

    A typed edge still counts. It is a real, checked relationship and
    `CONNECTIONS_SPEC.md` governs it — it is just not a thing a reader can
    click, which is why the two are now counted separately and
    `bin/wiki-lint` reports the gap.
    """
    linked = {p for p, _ in edges(fm)}
    linked.add(slug)
    for m in WIKILINK_RE.finditer(txt):
        linked.add(m.group(1))
    return linked


def scan_page(slug, pages, idx, matcher, limit=3, against=None):
    """Candidates for one page. Two modes, and they ask different questions.

    **Cited mode** (no `against`) reads the corpora the page's own `sources:`
    name. The page was assembled *from* this corpus, so every row in its date
    range is arguably its business, and the question is "what did the writer
    not carry across?"

    **Against mode** reads a corpus the page does NOT cite, and the date range
    alone is not enough to scope it: a person's page spanning 2009-2025 would
    match every entity in seventeen years of archive, which is the same answer
    for every such page and therefore no answer at all. So it first finds the
    rows that name **this page** — its own title, aliases and handles, at high
    confidence — and looks only inside those. The question becomes "in what
    this corpus says about X, what else does it name?", which is the question
    the source-mention obligation is actually about.

    A page whose names never appear is a fast, real negative: the corpus is
    silent on it, and that is worth knowing before anybody reads further.
    """
    path = slug + ".md"
    txt = pages[path]
    fm, _ = split_fm(txt)
    srcs = inline_list(fm, "sources")
    if against:
        corpora = list(against)
    else:
        corpora = [s for s in srcs if s in CORPORA]
    if not corpora:
        return None
    start, end = page_range(fm)
    # A missing date range used to refuse an --against scan. That was written
    # before the name filter below existed, when the range was the only thing
    # scoping the read; with the filter doing the scoping it blocked 246 pages
    # for nothing, and the rows it excluded — a page's subject named outside
    # the era the page covers — are among the more interesting results.
    # Unbounded now, and the output says so.

    linked = linked_targets(slug, fm, txt)

    rows, allhits, slug_rows, _dropped = corpus_index(corpora, matcher)

    if against:
        if not [n for n, c in idx[slug]["names"] if c == "high"]:
            return "NONAMES"
        # Only rows that name THIS page, and only on a high-confidence name of
        # it — a bare first name would drag in every row about anybody.
        candidate_rows = [i for i in slug_rows.get(slug, ())
                          if any(s == slug and c == "high"
                                 for _n, c, s in allhits.get(i, ()))]
    else:
        candidate_rows = sorted(allhits)

    hits = collections.defaultdict(list)
    matched_rows = 0
    for i in candidate_rows:
        ts, text, meta, base = rows[i]
        if start and ts[:10] < start:
            continue
        if end and ts[:10] > end:
            continue
        matched_rows += 1
        seen = set()
        for name, conf, tgt in allhits.get(i, ()):
            if tgt in linked or (name, tgt) in seen:
                continue
            seen.add((name, tgt))
            hits[tgt].append((ts[:10], name, conf, text, meta, base,
                              len(matcher.owner.get(name, ()))))
    if against:
        hits["__rows__"] = matched_rows
    return hits


def render_hits(slug, hits, idx, limit=3, show_low=False):
    """Candidates, ranked by how much the TARGET page needs them.

    Two orderings were tried. By mention count the head of the list is whatever
    string happens to recur — 48 SLOPPP shares, 18 hits on the word "music" —
    and the page whose own stated limit the source falsifies sits below the
    fold. By self-limit score first, the pages that told you what they do not
    know come first, which is where every finding of the pilot pass landed.
    """
    if not hits:
        print("  (nothing unlinked found)")
        return 0
    rows_matched = hits.pop("__rows__", None)
    if rows_matched is not None:
        print("  %d row(s) in this corpus name this page" % rows_matched)
        if rows_matched == 0:
            print("  (the corpus is silent on it — a real negative, worth recording)")
            return 0
    shown = {}
    suppressed = 0
    refused = 0
    for tgt, rows in hits.items():
        # THE MORATORIUM APPLIES TO A CANDIDATE TARGET, NOT ONLY TO THE PAGE
        # BEING SCANNED. It was enforced in two places — scanning her page, and
        # the --queue — and in neither of the two places where her page comes
        # back as somewhere to WRITE. A candidate list is a worklist whichever
        # column her name lands in. Found 2026-09-04 scanning the concert
        # record, which offered her page with 101 mentions.
        if MORATORIUM_TARGETS and tgt in MORATORIUM_TARGETS:
            refused += 1
            continue
        keep = rows if show_low else [r for r in rows if r[2] == "high"]
        if keep:
            shown[tgt] = keep
        else:
            suppressed += 1
    if not shown:
        print("  (nothing unlinked found%s)" % ("" if show_low else " above low confidence"))
        if suppressed:
            print("  %d low-confidence target(s) suppressed — --low to see them" % suppressed)
        return 0
    ranked = sorted(
        shown.items(),
        key=lambda kv: (-idx[kv[0]].get("limit", 0), -len(kv[1]), kv[0]),
    )
    for tgt, rows in ranked:
        conf = "high" if any(r[2] == "high" for r in rows) else "low"
        lim = idx[tgt].get("limit", 0)
        flag = ("  ** states %d limit(s) about its own evidence **" % lim) if lim else ""
        # A string two pages both claim is not evidence for either of them. The
        # index knows this and used to say nothing: @alexisarmel is in
        # alexis-armel's aliases AND in annie-ulmer's infobox handles, so 85
        # rows about one person were offered as 101 mentions of the other, with
        # no mark on the output to read them by.
        contested = sorted({r[1] for r in rows if r[6] > 1})
        if contested:
            flag += ("  !! %d name(s) contested by another page: %s"
                     % (len(contested), ", ".join(contested[:3])))
        phrases = sorted({r[1] for r in rows if r[1] in COMMON_PHRASE})
        if phrases:
            flag += ("  !! matched on ordinary English (%s) — THE COUNT IS NOT "
                     "EVIDENCE, read the rows" % ", ".join(phrases[:3]))
        print("  [%s] %-46s %d mention(s)  <%s>%s"
              % (conf, tgt, len(rows), idx[tgt]["domain"], flag))
        for date, tok, _c, text, meta, base, nown in rows[:limit]:
            snip = re.sub(r'\s+', ' ', text).strip()
            if len(snip) > 132:
                snip = snip[:129] + "..."
            # A truncated tweet permalink is 22 characters of "https://x.com/dan"
            # and tells a reader nothing; a message's direction and handle is
            # the whole point of reading it.
            m = "" if (meta or "").startswith("http") else (meta or "")[:22]
            print("        %s  %-16s %-22s %s" % (date, tok[:16], m, snip))
        if len(rows) > limit:
            print("        ... %d more" % (len(rows) - limit))
    if suppressed:
        print("  (%d low-confidence target(s) suppressed — --low to see them)" % suppressed)
    if refused:
        print("  (%d target(s) withheld under the standing directive in CLAUDE.md)"
              % refused)
    return len(ranked)


# Page types whose NAME a source could plausibly contain. A concept or
# synthesis page is named by a phrase this wiki coined — "the input clamp",
# "rooting for the fire" — and no corpus will ever hold it. Counting those
# against alias coverage is how Phase 0.5 got estimated at 389 pages when the
# real target is 281.
CORPUS_NAMEABLE = {"entity", "event", "place", "period", "report", "profile",
                   "summary", "dataset"}


def cmd_entities_audit(idx, pages):
    """Are the aliases we already have any good?

    `entities --missing` asks which pages have no aliases. This asks the other
    question, and it turns out to matter more, because a wrong alias is worse
    than a missing one: a missing alias makes the index blind, and an alias that
    is also ordinary English makes it **confidently wrong**.

    Measured 2026-09-04 over 108 pages and 326 declared aliases:

        233  appear ZERO times in any readable corpus
          6  are ordinary English and therefore match constantly
         87  work

    **The 233 is not a defect and reporting it as one would be the mistake.**
    `aliases:` does two different jobs here. One is *what the record calls this
    thing* — `o&a`, `@OpieRadio`, `Lex` — which is what a corpus scan needs. The
    other is *what a reader might call this page* — `the input clamp`, `the
    unevaluated domain` — which is navigation, and which no corpus can contain
    by construction. A zero-hit alias is doing the second job, matches nothing,
    and costs nothing. Leave it alone.

    The six in the middle bucket are the whole problem. `the case` on a concept
    page returned 96 rows and **not one of them was the concept**; `the drive`
    returned 48; `the Dude` returned 18 and every one was generic English. They
    are reported at HIGH confidence because they are multi-token, which is the
    confidence rule failing exactly where it is trusted most.
    """
    rels = sorted(CORPORA)
    matcher = Matcher(idx)
    mark_common_phrases(idx, [r for r in rels
                              if CORPORA[r].get("family") == "messages"])
    declared = {}
    for path, txt in pages.items():
        fm, _ = split_fm(txt)
        al = inline_list(fm, "aliases")
        if al:
            declared[path[:-3]] = (al, scalar(fm, "page_type") or "?")
    names = {a.lower() for al, _pt in declared.values() for a in al}
    trig = collections.defaultdict(list)
    for a in names:
        toks = [t for t in TOKEN_RE.findall(a) if t]
        if toks:
            trig[max(toks, key=len)].append(a)
    counts = collections.Counter()
    for rel in rels:
        for _ts, text, _m in load_corpus(rel):
            low = text.lower()
            for k in set(TOKEN_RE.findall(low)) & trig.keys():
                for a in trig[k]:
                    if a in low:
                        counts[a] += 1
    harmful, inert, working = [], [], []
    for slug, (al, pt) in sorted(declared.items()):
        for a in al:
            k = a.lower()
            n = counts[k]
            if k in COMMON_PHRASE and n:
                harmful.append((n, slug, a, pt))
            elif not n:
                inert.append((slug, a, pt))
            else:
                working.append((n, slug, a, pt))
    harmful.sort(reverse=True)
    print("HARMFUL — ordinary English, matches constantly, none of it evidence")
    print("  %-46s %-24s %8s" % ("page", "alias", "hits"))
    for n, slug, a, _pt in harmful:
        print("  %-46s %-24s %8d" % (slug[:46], a[:24], n))
    print("  Read the rows before deleting: one of these was correct on the "
          "last pass\n  (`Google AI` really is how he writes about Gemini).")
    entity_inert = [r for r in inert if r[2] in CORPUS_NAMEABLE]
    print("\nINERT — zero hits anywhere: %d, of which %d sit on a page type a "
          "corpus could name" % (len(inert), len(entity_inert)))
    print("  On a concept or synthesis page this is CORRECT — the alias is "
          "navigation, not a\n  corpus name, and it costs nothing. On an entity "
          "page it is worth a look:")
    for slug, a, pt in entity_inert[:20]:
        print("  %-46s %-24s <%s>" % (slug[:46], a[:24], pt))
    if len(entity_inert) > 20:
        print("  ... %d more" % (len(entity_inert) - 20))
    print("\nWORKING — %d alias(es) matching real rows" % len(working))
    print("\n%d page(s) declare %d alias(es): %d harmful, %d inert, %d working"
          % (len(declared), len(names), len(harmful), len(inert), len(working)))
    return 0


def cmd_entities_missing(idx, pages):
    """Pages carrying no alias, so the index can only find them by exact title.

    Ranked by how likely that is to cost something: a page whose title is a
    single token is invisible anyway (it scores `low` and is suppressed), and a
    page whose title is a multi-word proper noun is exactly the kind a source
    abbreviates. 395 of 497 pages are in here as of 2026-09-04.
    """
    rows = []
    for slug in sorted(idx):
        fm, _ = split_fm(pages[slug + ".md"])
        if inline_list(fm, "aliases"):
            continue
        title = idx[slug]["title"]
        toks = len(title.split())
        # A multi-word title is abbreviated in sources; a one-word title is
        # already unfindable for a different reason. Both need aliases, and the
        # first kind is where a scan is silently returning a false negative.
        rows.append((0 if toks > 1 else 1, -idx[slug].get("limit", 0), slug, title, toks))
    rows.sort()
    for kind, neglim, slug, title, toks in rows:
        tag = "multi-word title, likely abbreviated in sources" if kind == 0 else "single token"
        lim = -neglim
        print("  %-52s %-34s %s%s" % (slug[:52], title[:34], tag,
                                      ("  ** %d self-limit claim(s) **" % lim) if lim else ""))
    print("\n%d page(s) carry no aliases: — the index can only match their exact title"
          % len(rows))
    return 0


def cmd_scan(argv):
    pages = load_pages()
    note_moratorium_targets(pages)
    idx = build_index(pages)
    matcher = Matcher(idx)
    mark_common_phrases(idx, [r for r in sorted(CORPORA)
                              if CORPORA[r].get("family") == "messages"])

    show_low = "--low" in argv
    argv = [a for a in argv if a != "--low"]
    conversation = "--conversation" in argv
    argv = [a for a in argv if a != "--conversation"]
    against = None
    if "--against" in argv:
        i = argv.index("--against")
        if i + 1 >= len(argv):
            print("scan: --against needs a corpus path", file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        corpus = argv[i + 1]
        if corpus not in CORPORA:
            print("scan: no reader for %s\n  readable: %s"
                  % (corpus, ", ".join(sorted(CORPORA))), file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        against = [corpus]
        argv = argv[:i] + argv[i + 2:]

    targets = []
    if argv and argv[0] == "--all":
        targets = [s for s in sorted(idx) if s.startswith("wiki/self/twitter")]
    elif argv and argv[0] == "--queue":
        # The campaign queue: pages that state a limit about their own evidence,
        # loudest first. Requires --against, because the point of the queue is
        # to check a page against a corpus it does NOT already cite.
        if not against:
            print("scan --queue: needs --against <corpus> — the queue exists to "
                  "check a page against a corpus it does not already cite",
                  file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        n = int(argv[1]) if len(argv) > 1 and argv[1].isdigit() else 25
        scored = []
        for slug, meta in idx.items():
            if not meta.get("limit"):
                continue
            if under_moratorium(pages[slug + ".md"], slug + ".md"):
                continue
            fm, _ = split_fm(pages[slug + ".md"])
            if against[0] in inline_list(fm, "sources"):
                continue          # already cites it; ordinary scan covers that
            scored.append((-meta["limit"], slug))
        targets = [s for _, s in sorted(scored)][:n]
        if not targets:
            print("scan --queue: nothing eligible for %s" % against[0])
            return 0
    elif argv:
        for a in argv:
            s = a[:-3] if a.endswith(".md") else a
            if s + ".md" not in pages:
                print("scan: no such page: %s" % a, file=sys.stderr)
                return 1
            targets.append(s)
    else:
        print(USAGE)
        return 1

    total, skipped, refused = 0, 0, 0
    for slug in targets:
        if under_moratorium(pages[slug + ".md"], slug + ".md"):
            print("== %s — REFUSED: standing directive in CLAUDE.md. This tool "
                  "hands out pages to write edges onto; that record does not "
                  "advance." % slug)
            refused += 1
            continue
        _m = mentions_moratorium(pages[slug + ".md"])
        if _m:
            print("   ! this page names her %d time(s). Scanning it is fine; the "
                  "directive governs what you WRITE — no new claim about her, "
                  "on this page or any other." % _m)
        if conversation:
            corpora = against or [r for r in sorted(CORPORA)
                                  if CORPORA[r].get("family") == "messages"]
            hits = scan_conversation(slug, pages, idx, matcher, corpora)
            if isinstance(hits, tuple) and hits[0] == "NOHANDLE":
                amb = hits[1]
                print("== %s — no counterparty handle resolves into %s"
                      % (slug, ", ".join(os.path.basename(c) for c in corpora)))
                for raw, n in amb:
                    print("   ! masked handle %s matches %d corpus handles — "
                          "not guessed at" % (raw, n))
                skipped += 1
                continue
            hs = hits.pop("__handles__", [])
            nrows = hits.pop("__rows__", 0)
            print("== %s  [conversation with %s]" % (slug, ", ".join(hs)))
            print("  %d row(s) in the conversation" % nrows)
            if not nrows:
                print("  (the corpus holds no messages with this counterparty "
                      "— a real negative, worth recording)")
                continue
            total += render_hits(slug, hits, idx, show_low=show_low)
            continue
        hits = scan_page(slug, pages, idx, matcher, against=against)
        if hits is None:
            print("== %s — cites no corpus this tool can read" % slug)
            continue
        if hits == "NONAMES":
            print("== %s — SKIPPED: no unambiguous name to search for (a bare "
                  "first name would match anybody)" % slug)
            skipped += 1
            continue
        _fm2, _ = split_fm(pages[slug + ".md"])
        _s2, _e2 = page_range(_fm2)
        _scope = ""
        if against:
            _scope = ("  [against %s%s]"
                      % (os.path.basename(against[0]),
                         "" if (_s2 and _e2) else ", UNBOUNDED — page has no date range"))
        print("== %s%s" % (slug, _scope))
        total += render_hits(slug, hits, idx, show_low=show_low)
    bits = []
    if skipped:
        bits.append("%d unscannable (no date range, or no unambiguous name)" % skipped)
    if refused:
        bits.append("%d refused under the standing directive" % refused)
    tail = (", " + ", ".join(bits)) if bits else ""
    print("\n%d candidate edge(s) across %d page(s)%s — verify each by reading "
          "the rows above" % (total, len(targets) - skipped - refused, tail))
    return 0


def cmd_coverage(argv):
    """Which readable corpora actually name a page, and how loudly.

    Built after the first queue run, where ten of twelve pages came back with
    the corpus silent on them. That is a correct and cheap answer, but paying
    a full scan to get it is the wrong order of operations: matching a page to
    the corpus that has something to say about it is the triage step, and this
    is it. Rows, not candidates.

    Each corpus is indexed once and every page reads its own rows out of that
    index. The first version ran the whole corpus once per page, which was
    invisible at 1.3 MB and would have been 497 passes over 217,573 records the
    moment the message dump became readable.

    `--silent` inverts it: the pages a corpus does NOT name. On a corpus a page
    cites, that is the interesting half — the page claims a source that never
    mentions it, which is either a missing alias or a citation doing no work.
    """
    pages = load_pages()
    note_moratorium_targets(pages)
    idx = build_index(pages)
    matcher = Matcher(idx)
    mark_common_phrases(idx, [r for r in sorted(CORPORA)
                              if CORPORA[r].get("family") == "messages"])
    only = [a for a in argv if not a.startswith("-")]
    silent = "--silent" in argv
    prefix = only[0] if only else "wiki/"
    per_corpus = {}
    for rel in sorted(CORPORA):
        rows, hits, slug_rows, _d = corpus_index([rel], matcher)
        per_corpus[rel] = (rows, hits, slug_rows)
    out, quiet = [], []
    for slug in sorted(idx):
        if not slug.startswith(prefix.rstrip("/")):
            continue
        txt = pages[slug + ".md"]
        if under_moratorium(txt, slug + ".md"):
            continue
        fm, _ = split_fm(txt)
        if not [n for n, c in idx[slug]["names"] if c == "high"]:
            continue
        start, end = page_range(fm)
        cited = inline_list(fm, "sources")
        per = {}
        for rel, (rows, hits, slug_rows) in per_corpus.items():
            n = 0
            for i in slug_rows.get(slug, ()):
                if not any(s == slug and c == "high" for _nm, c, s in hits.get(i, ())):
                    continue
                ts = rows[i][0]
                if start and ts[:10] < start:
                    continue
                if end and ts[:10] > end:
                    continue
                n += 1
            per[rel] = (n, rel in cited)
        best = max((v[0] for v in per.values()), default=0)
        if best:
            out.append((best, slug, per, idx[slug].get("limit", 0)))
        else:
            quiet.append((slug, [r for r in cited if r in CORPORA],
                          idx[slug].get("limit", 0),
                          scalar(fm, "page_type") or "?"))
    if silent:
        # A synthesis or concept page is named by no corpus BY CONSTRUCTION —
        # "block unblock loop" is a finding this wiki coined, not a phrase
        # anybody texted. Reporting those beside a person the corpus really is
        # silent about would make the number mean two different things at once,
        # so the type is printed and the tail counts them apart.
        COINED = {"synthesis", "concept"}
        quiet.sort(key=lambda r: (-r[2], r[0]))
        n_cited = n_coined = 0
        for slug, cites, lim, ptype in quiet:
            tag = ("  cites %s and is not named in it"
                   % ",".join(os.path.basename(c) for c in cites)) if cites else ""
            if ptype in COINED:
                n_coined += 1
            elif cites:
                n_cited += 1
            print("%-52s %6d  %-10s%s" % (slug[:52], lim, ptype[:10], tag))
        print("\n%d page(s) no readable corpus names. %d are synthesis or concept "
              "pages, whose names the corpus cannot contain; %d of the rest cite a "
              "corpus anyway — a missing alias, or a citation doing no work"
              % (len(quiet), n_coined, n_cited))
        return 0
    out.sort(key=lambda r: (-r[3], -r[0]))
    print("%-52s %6s %6s  %s" % ("page", "rows", "limits", "corpus (* = already cited)"))
    for best, slug, per, lim in out:
        for rel, (n, cites) in sorted(per.items(), key=lambda kv: -kv[1][0]):
            if n:
                print("%-52s %6d %6d  %s%s"
                      % (slug[:52], n, lim, os.path.basename(rel), "*" if cites else ""))
    print("\n%d page(s) named by at least one readable corpus, "
          "self-limit claims first; %d named by none (--silent)"
          % (len(out), len(quiet)))
    print("An unstarred row is a page a corpus talks about and that does not "
          "cite it — the crosslink queue proper.")
    return 0


def cmd_handles(argv):
    """Which pages can be joined to a message corpus by counterparty.

    The message-corpus analogue of `entities --missing`, and the same kind of
    bottleneck. A page that declares a phone number or an address can have the
    whole conversation with that person read for what it names; a page that
    declares none can only be found by somebody writing its name in a message,
    which on a five-word-average corpus is rare and unreliable.

    Measured 2026-09-04 over `wiki/people/`: 29 pages resolve and reach 35,326
    rows between them; **136 carry no `handles:` field at all.**
    """
    pages = load_pages()
    note_moratorium_targets(pages)
    only = [a for a in argv if not a.startswith("-")]
    prefix = only[0] if only else "wiki/people/"
    corpora = [r for r in sorted(CORPORA) if CORPORA[r].get("family") == "messages"]
    known = corpus_handles(corpora)
    resolved, unresolved, none = [], [], []
    for path, txt in sorted(pages.items()):
        slug = path[:-3]
        if not slug.startswith(prefix.rstrip("/")):
            continue
        fm, _ = split_fm(txt)
        declared = [h for h in inline_list(fm, "handles") if not h.startswith("@")]
        hits, amb = resolve_handles(fm, known)
        if hits:
            resolved.append((sum(known[h] for h in hits), slug, hits,
                             under_moratorium(txt, slug + ".md")))
        elif declared or amb:
            unresolved.append((slug, declared, amb))
        else:
            none.append(slug)
    resolved.sort(reverse=True)
    dupes = collections.defaultdict(list)
    for _n, slug, hits, _m in resolved:
        for h in hits:
            dupes[h].append(slug)
    print("%6s  %-46s %s" % ("rows", "page", "handle(s)"))
    for n, slug, hits, mor in resolved:
        tag = "  [under the standing directive — scan refuses it]" if mor else ""
        print("%6d  %-46s %s%s" % (n, slug[:46], ",".join(hits)[:38], tag))
    shared = {h: s for h, s in dupes.items() if len(s) > 1}
    if shared:
        print("\n%d handle(s) claimed by more than one page — one of each pair is "
              "wrong, or they are the same person twice:" % len(shared))
        for h, slugs in sorted(shared.items()):
            print("  %-18s %s" % (h, ", ".join(slugs)))
    if unresolved:
        print("\n%d page(s) declare a handle that is not in the corpus:" % len(unresolved))
        for slug, declared, amb in unresolved[:20]:
            note = ""
            if amb:
                note = "  (masked, matches %d — not guessed at)" % amb[0][1]
            print("  %-46s %s%s" % (slug[:46], ",".join(declared)[:30], note))
    print("\n%d page(s) resolve and reach %d row(s); %d declare an unmatched handle; "
          "**%d carry no handles: at all** — the message corpus's blind spot, and "
          "the same shape as `entities --missing`"
          % (len(resolved), sum(n for n, _s, _h, _m in resolved),
             len(unresolved), len(none)))
    return 0


MSG_COUNT_RE = re.compile(r'^\|\s*Messages\s*\|\s*\**~?([\d,]+)(.*)$', re.M)
MSG_DIR_RE = re.compile(r'^\|\s*Direction\s*\|\s*(.+?)\s*\|', re.M)
# A count line naming a different corpus is not comparable to the iMessage
# figure and must not be reported as wrong. christo-coan's "~50 (Facebook
# Messenger, both directions)" is correct and the first version of this check
# called it a 44-message error.
OTHER_CORPUS = re.compile(r'facebook|messenger|instagram|gmail|google chat|email', re.I)


def cmd_counts(argv):
    """Does a page's stated message count match the corpus it is drawn from?

    **The defect this exists for has been found four times by hand and never by
    a tool.** A page states `| Messages | 41 |` and, often, `| Direction | All
    received (export artifact) |` — and the second line is the tell. Dan's half
    of the thread is not missing from the record; it is in the corpus the page
    did not read. `wiki/people/bruce-burish` carries a correction saying "181 is
    exactly the received count", `wiki/people/zach-clingan` says "22 is exactly"
    the same thing, and on 2026-09-04 `rod-banks` (41 stated, 92 actual) and
    `zach-hendricks` (58 stated, 65 actual) made it four.

    Measured the same day: **14 pages disagree with the corpus, 9 of them by
    exactly the Received-only figure.** `wiki/people/vaughn` states 228 against
    582 — 354 of Dan's own messages uncounted.

    This is not a gate. The count on a page can legitimately be a subset, a
    different corpus, or a figure from a source this tool cannot read, so it
    prints and exits 0 like every other reading job here.
    """
    pages = load_pages()
    rels = [r for r in sorted(CORPORA) if CORPORA[r].get("family") == "messages"]
    known = corpus_handles(rels)
    per_handle = collections.defaultdict(collections.Counter)
    seen = set()
    for rel in rels:
        for ts, text, meta in load_corpus(rel):
            k = dedupe_key(ts, text)
            if k in seen:
                continue
            seen.add(k)
            p = (meta or "").split(" ", 1)
            if len(p) == 2 and p[1] and p[1] != "-":
                per_handle[p[1]][p[0]] += 1
    only = [a for a in argv if not a.startswith("-")]
    prefix = only[0] if only else "wiki/people/"
    rows, over, skipped = [], [], 0
    for path, txt in sorted(pages.items()):
        slug = path[:-3]
        if not slug.startswith(prefix.rstrip("/")):
            continue
        fm, _ = split_fm(txt)
        hits, _amb = resolve_handles(fm, known)
        if not hits:
            continue
        m = MSG_COUNT_RE.search(txt)
        if not m:
            continue
        if OTHER_CORPUS.search(m.group(2) or ""):
            skipped += 1
            continue
        stated = int(m.group(1).replace(",", ""))
        c = collections.Counter()
        for h in hits:
            c.update(per_handle[h])
        total, sent, recv = sum(c.values()), c["Sent"], c["Received"]
        d = MSG_DIR_RE.search(txt)
        dirn = d.group(1).strip() if d else ""
        note = ""
        if stated == recv and sent:
            note = "STATED = RECEIVED-ONLY, %d sent uncounted" % sent
        elif stated < total - max(3, 0.15 * total):
            note = "understates by %d" % (total - stated)
        if note:
            rows.append((total - stated, slug, stated, total, sent, recv, dirn, note))
        elif stated > total + max(3, 0.15 * total):
            over.append((stated - total, slug, stated, total))
    rows.sort(reverse=True)
    over.sort(reverse=True)
    print("%-40s %7s %7s %6s %6s  %s"
          % ("page", "stated", "actual", "sent", "recv", "what is wrong"))
    for _d, slug, stated, total, sent, recv, dirn, note in rows:
        tag = "  [%s]" % dirn[:34] if dirn else ""
        print("%-40s %7d %7d %6d %6d  %s%s"
              % (slug[:40], stated, total, sent, recv, note, tag))
    print("\n%d page(s) UNDERSTATE against the corpus; %d skipped for naming a "
          "corpus this tool cannot read" % (len(rows), skipped))
    print("A page saying 'all received (export artifact)' is the tell: his half "
          "is not\nmissing from the record, it is in the corpus the page did not "
          "read.")
    if over:
        # An overstatement is usually NOT an error and must not be reported as
        # one. This tool reads two corpora; `raw/self/message-csv/` also holds
        # ~30 per-contact exports it does not, and 50,396 rows exist only in the
        # CSV side of the family. A page that counted a per-contact export will
        # legitimately state more than these two hold.
        print("\n%d page(s) state MORE than these two corpora hold. That is "
              "usually not an\nerror: ~30 per-contact exports under "
              "raw/self/message-csv/ are unread here." % len(over))
        for d, slug, stated, total in over:
            print("  %-40s stated %6d, these corpora hold %6d  (+%d)"
                  % (slug[:40], stated, total, d))
    return 0


def cmd_reciprocal(argv):
    pages = load_pages()
    filt = argv[0] if argv else None
    fms = {p: split_fm(t)[0] for p, t in pages.items()}
    debt = []
    generated = []
    for path, fm in sorted(fms.items()):
        slug = path[:-3]
        if filt and not slug.startswith(filt.rstrip("/")):
            continue
        for tgt, typ in edges(fm):
            if typ in NO_INVERSE:
                continue
            tf = fms.get(tgt + ".md")
            if tf is None:
                continue  # wiki-connect check owns unresolvable targets
            if tgt in GENERATED:
                generated.append((slug, typ, tgt))
                continue
            back = [t for p, t in edges(tf) if p == slug]
            if not back:
                debt.append((slug, typ, tgt, "no edge back"))
            else:
                want = INVERSE.get(typ, typ if typ in SYMMETRIC else None)
                if want and want not in back:
                    debt.append((slug, typ, tgt, "back-edge is '%s', expected '%s'" % (back[0], want)))
    for s, typ, t, why in debt:
        print("  %-44s --%s--> %-44s  %s" % (s, typ, t, why))
    print("\n%d edge(s) owe an inverse" % len(debt))
    if generated:
        print("\n%d edge(s) point at a GENERATED page and are not reciprocable by hand:" % len(generated))
        for s, typ, t in generated:
            print("  %-44s --%s--> %s" % (s, typ, t))
        print("  (a hand-added inverse there is destroyed on the next generator run)")
    return 0


def cmd_orphaned(argv):
    pages = load_pages()
    out = []
    for path, txt in sorted(pages.items()):
        fm, _ = split_fm(txt)
        srcs = [s for s in inline_list(fm, "sources") if s in CORPORA]
        if not srcs:
            continue
        n = len(edges(fm))
        out.append((n, path[:-3], srcs))
    for n, slug, srcs in sorted(out):
        flag = "  <-- no edges" if n == 0 else ""
        print("  %3d edges  %-52s %s%s" % (n, slug, ",".join(os.path.basename(s) for s in srcs), flag))
    print("\n%d page(s) cite a readable corpus" % len(out))
    return 0


def cmd_check(argv):
    pages = load_pages()
    fms = {p: split_fm(t)[0] for p, t in pages.items()}
    debt = 0
    for path, fm in fms.items():
        slug = path[:-3]
        for tgt, typ in edges(fm):
            if typ in NO_INVERSE:
                continue
            tf = fms.get(tgt + ".md")
            if tf is None or tgt in GENERATED:
                continue
            if not any(p == slug for p, _ in edges(tf)):
                debt += 1
    if debt:
        print("crosslink: %d one-way edge(s) owe an inverse — bin/wiki-crosslink reciprocal" % debt)
    else:
        print("crosslink: every typed edge is reciprocated")
    return 0


USAGE = """usage: wiki-crosslink <entities|coverage|handles|counts|scan|unlinked|breadcrumb|
                       reciprocal|orphaned|check> [args]

  entities [filter]          the strings a scan can look for
  entities --missing         pages with no aliases: — the index's blind spot
  entities --audit           are the aliases we HAVE any good? harmful (ordinary
                             English, matches constantly) / inert (zero hits,
                             fine on a concept page) / working
  handles [prefix]           which pages join to a message corpus by
                             counterparty, and which cannot — the message
                             corpus's own blind spot
  scan <page>|--all          unlinked entities in a page's own cited corpora
  scan <page> --conversation everything named inside the CONVERSATION with this
                             page's counterparty — the question a message
                             corpus can answer and a broadcast archive cannot
  scan --against <corpus>    ...against a corpus the page does NOT cite,
                             filtered to rows that name the page itself
  scan --queue [n] --against the campaign queue: pages that state a limit
                             about their own evidence, loudest first
       [--low]               include single-token name matches (mostly noise)
  unlinked [page …]          names a page writes IN ITS OWN PROSE and never
                             links — the reader-facing half, wiki against wiki,
                             no corpus read. Body [[wikilinks]] only: a typed
                             edge is frontmatter and nobody can click it
       [--low]               include single-token matches (mostly noise)
       [--apply]             write the first safe occurrence of each
                             unambiguous name. Refuses low confidence, more
                             than one owning page, and a phrase said by more
                             than 20 pages; never writes into a quotation, a
                             heading, code, or an existing link
  breadcrumb [page …]        pages that never link the index above them — the
                             other half of STYLE_GUIDE's reachability rule,
                             which only ever said "reachable FROM"
       [--apply]             write the **Up:** footer (ancestor chain, plus
                             previous/next where the siblings are a numeric
                             run). Idempotent; rerun rather than hand-editing
  rederive                   pages that admit their own prose predates half
                             their evidence — the debt `counts` created and
                             could not measure, biggest thread first
  coverage [prefix]          which readable corpora name a page, and how often
                             — the triage step, run this before scanning
       [--silent]            invert it: pages no corpus names, and which of
                             them cite one anyway
  counts [prefix]            pages whose stated message count disagrees with
                             the corpus — usually a Received-only figure
  reciprocal [prefix]        typed edges whose target does not link back
  orphaned                   corpus-backed pages and their edge counts
  check                      the reciprocal-debt banner (always exits 0)
"""


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- unlinked

PROTECTED_RE = re.compile(
    r"```.*?```"                          # a fenced code block
    r"|^\s{0,3}#{1,6}[^\n]*"              # a heading line
    r"|^[ \t]*>[^\n]*"                    # a blockquote line
    r"|\[\[[^\]]*\]\]"                   # an existing wikilink
    r"|`[^`\n]*`"                         # inline code
    r"|\[[^\]\n]*\]\([^)\n]*\)"         # a markdown link
    r"|\u201c[^\u201d]{0,4000}\u201d"     # a curly-quoted span
    r'|"[^"]{0,4000}"',                   # a straight-quoted span
    re.S | re.M)


def protected_spans(body):
    """Character ranges of the body a link must not be written into.

    **Computed over the whole body rather than line by line, and that is the
    entire point.** The first version of this masked each line on its own and
    wrote a wikilink into the middle of a quoted tweet:

        nothingness but at least I got the [[wiki/self/twitter|@danfrank]] handle"*

    The opening quote was two lines up. Prose here is hard-wrapped at ~78
    columns, so a quotation of any length spans lines by default and a
    per-line mask sees only its tail — the one case the mask exists for is the
    one it could not see. Over-masking is the safe direction: a missed link
    costs a click, an edited quotation costs the record.

    Blockquotes and headings are spans here for the same reason: the corpus
    quotes its sources verbatim (`STYLE_GUIDE.md` rule 9 keeps superseded
    claims visible inside them), and heading text is what the site turns into
    anchors.
    """
    return [m.span() for m in PROTECTED_RE.finditer(body)]


PHRASE_PAGE_CAP = 20


def overexposed(targets, idx, matcher, pages):
    """Names that turn out to be phrases, by how much of the wiki says them.

    `mark_common_phrases` catches a title that is ordinary English *in the
    message corpus*. It cannot catch one that is ordinary English **in the
    wiki's own register**, because it never reads the wiki — and that is where
    this operation does its matching. Measured on the first corpus-wide run:

      "the wiki"       -> wiki/self/concepts/wiki-brain   **104 pages**
      "@danfrank"      -> wiki/self/twitter                  24 pages
      "the repository" -> wiki/self/concepts/wiki-brain        6 pages
      "Cobra Starship" -> .../artists/cobra-starship          11 pages
      "Seven Springs"  -> wiki/places/seven-springs           10 pages

    The first is not a name in those hundred sentences. "the wiki records 17
    February" means *this document*, and a link there sends the reader to a
    page about the project to learn nothing. The last two are names, said by
    ten pages because ten pages are about them. **Dispersion separates them
    cleanly and nothing else tried did** — the phrases are lowercase and so are
    good descriptive link texts ("the [[intake ledger]]"), so case does not.

    Dispersion failed as a discriminator over the message corpus, where 504
    handles are mostly a handful of messages each and even "say" reaches 14.7%
    of them. Here the unit is a page written on purpose, and it works.

    **Only an alias can be refused this way.** A page's own title said on
    fifty pages is a subject the wiki keeps returning to; an *alias* said on
    fifty pages is a phrase somebody put in an `aliases:` list. The first cut
    of this refused `fall out boy` — a real band on twenty-one pages, because
    he is a fan — alongside `the wiki`, and the two are only separable here:
    `Fall Out Boy` is what its page is called, `the wiki` is the fourth entry
    in another page's alias list. Dispersion says how loud a string is; title
    versus alias says whether it is a name.

    Refused rather than demoted: a phrase this widespread wants one editorial
    decision about where the link belongs, not 104 mechanical ones.
    """
    primary = set()
    for slug, meta in idx.items():
        primary.add(meta["title"].lower())
        primary.add(os.path.basename(slug).replace("-", " ").lower())
    seen = collections.defaultdict(set)
    for slug in targets:
        txt = pages[slug + ".md"]
        _fm, body = split_fm(txt)
        spans = protected_spans(body)
        low = body.lower()
        for name, conf in matcher.find(body):
            if conf != "high":
                continue
            if any(not any(m.start() < y and x < m.end() for x, y in spans)
                   for m in matcher._boundary(name).finditer(low)):
                seen[name].add(slug)
    return {n for n, ps in seen.items()
            if len(ps) > PHRASE_PAGE_CAP and n not in primary}


def apply_unlinked(slug, txt, idx, matcher, refuse=frozenset()):
    """Link the first safe prose occurrence of each unambiguous name.

    Refuses everything it cannot be sure of, because the failure mode is a
    confident wrong link on a public page and there are a thousand of these:

    - `low` confidence — a one-token name matches anybody, and the corpus has
      a band called HIM and a page called Wiki;
    - more than one owning page — `the third party` is two people;
    - a name flagged by `mark_common_phrases` — ordinary English that happens
      to be a page title;
    - a target the page already links anywhere in the body. **First mention
      only**: the same name linked forty times down a 94KB page is a different
      kind of unreadable.

    Longest name first, so `judge fred adams` is consumed before `fred adams`
    can match inside it, and every write extends the mask.
    """
    fm, body = split_fm(txt)
    head = txt[:len(txt) - len(body)]
    own = {n.lower() for n, _c in idx.get(slug, {}).get("names", [])}
    taken = set(WIKILINK_RE.findall(txt)) | {slug}

    cands = {}
    for name, conf in matcher.find(body):
        if conf != "high" or name in own or name in refuse:
            continue
        owners = [o for o in sorted(matcher.owner.get(name, ()))
                  if o != slug and o in idx]
        if len(owners) != 1 or owners[0] in taken:
            continue
        if under_moratorium("", owners[0] + ".md"):
            continue
        cands[name] = owners[0]

    spans = protected_spans(body)
    low = body.lower()
    edits, done = [], set()
    for name in sorted(cands, key=len, reverse=True):
        tgt = cands[name]
        if tgt in done:
            continue
        # **A common phrase is demoted here rather than refused, and only
        # here.** `mark_common_phrases` is calibrated on the message corpus,
        # where "say anything" is 134 rows of people saying it and 0 rows about
        # the band. This operation matches the wiki's own prose, which is
        # deliberate writing, so the same flag would throw away five real bands
        # — Fall Out Boy, A Day To Remember, Taking Back Sunday, The Starting
        # Line, Hey Monday — to catch "the office". Case separates them: the
        # wiki writes the band as `Say Anything` and the room as `the office`,
        # so a flagged name must appear in exactly the form its own page uses.
        exact = idx[tgt]["title"] if name in COMMON_PHRASE else None
        for m in matcher._boundary(name).finditer(low):
            a, b = m.span()
            if exact is not None and body[a:b] != exact:
                continue
            if any(a < y and x < b for x, y in spans):
                continue
            edits.append((a, b, tgt))
            spans.append((a, b))
            done.add(tgt)
            break

    if not edits:
        return txt, []
    applied = []
    for a, b, tgt in sorted(edits, reverse=True):
        applied.append((body[a:b].lower(), tgt))
        body = body[:a] + "[[%s|%s]]" % (tgt, body[a:b]) + body[b:]
    return head + body, sorted(applied)


def _line_index(body):
    starts = [0] + [i + 1 for i, c in enumerate(body) if c == "\n"]

    def at(pos):
        return bisect.bisect_right(starts, pos)
    return at


def unlinked_names(slug, fm, txt, idx, matcher):
    """Entities this page NAMES IN ITS OWN PROSE and does not link.

    **A different question from `scan`, and cheaper than it by three orders of
    magnitude.** `scan` reads a source corpus and asks what a page never
    learned — that needs the corpus, a date range, and somebody to read the
    rows, because a mention in a message is not yet a fact about anybody. This
    asks only whether a name the page has *already written, in its own voice,
    deliberately* is something the reader can click. There is no source to read
    and no judgement about relevance: the page decided the entity mattered when
    it typed the name.

    Which is why it went unmeasured for the whole campaign. Every instrument
    here pointed outward at `raw/`, and the defect the operator actually hit
    was inward — `wiki/self/twitter/2013.md` is 94KB of the wiki's own prose
    carrying **nine** links.

    Counts only what `--apply` would consider, so the census and the write
    cannot disagree: same mask, same confidence rule, same ownership test.
    """
    # **Body wikilinks only — a typed edge does not count here**, which is the
    # opposite of `linked_targets`' rule and deliberately so. That function
    # answers "does this page assert a relationship?", where an edge is a real
    # answer. This one answers "can the reader get there?", where an edge is
    # frontmatter and the reader never sees it. Counting edges as links is what
    # let 161 of them stand in for zero navigation across the twitter tree.
    _fm, body = split_fm(txt)
    linked = set(WIKILINK_RE.findall(txt)) | {slug}
    own = {n.lower() for n, _c in idx.get(slug, {}).get("names", [])}
    spans = protected_spans(body)
    low = body.lower()
    line_at = _line_index(body)

    hits = collections.OrderedDict()
    for name, conf in matcher.find(body):
        if name in own:
            continue
        owners = [o for o in sorted(matcher.owner.get(name, ()))
                  if o != slug and o in idx and o not in linked]
        if not owners:
            continue
        if any(under_moratorium("", o + ".md") for o in owners):
            continue
        free = [m.span() for m in matcher._boundary(name).finditer(low)
                if not any(m.start() < y and x < m.end() for x, y in spans)]
        if not free:
            continue
        pos = free[0][0]
        eol = body.find("\n", pos)
        hits[(name, tuple(owners))] = {
            "conf": conf,
            "n": len(free),
            "first": (line_at(pos),
                      body[body.rfind("\n", 0, pos) + 1:
                           eol if eol != -1 else len(body)].strip()),
        }
    return hits


def cmd_unlinked(argv):
    """Names a page writes in its own prose but never links."""
    args = [a for a in argv if not a.startswith("-")]
    flags = {a for a in argv if a.startswith("-")}
    show_low = "--low" in flags
    do_apply = "--apply" in flags
    pages = load_pages()
    note_moratorium_targets(pages)
    idx = build_index(pages)
    matcher = Matcher(idx)
    if do_apply:
        # The common-phrase filter is only populated by a corpus pass, and
        # `--apply` refuses on it. Running without it would silently drop the
        # one guard that catches "say anything" and "the office".
        mark_common_phrases(idx, [r for r in CORPORA if os.path.exists(r)])

    if args:
        targets = []
        for a in args:
            s = a[:-3] if a.endswith(".md") else a
            if s + ".md" in pages:
                targets.append(s)
            else:
                print(f"no such page: {a}", file=sys.stderr)
                return 1
    else:
        targets = sorted(s for s in idx if not is_generated_slug(s))

    if do_apply:
        live = [s for s in targets
                if not under_moratorium(pages[s + ".md"], s + ".md")]
        # Over the WHOLE corpus, never over the pages named on the command
        # line. Dispersion is a fact about how the wiki uses a string, so
        # scoping it to one page would make `--apply <page>` write links that
        # `--apply` refuses — the tool disagreeing with itself depending on how
        # it was invoked, which is how a mechanical pass stops being trustable.
        everything = [s for s in idx
                      if not is_generated_slug(s)
                      and not under_moratorium(pages[s + ".md"], s + ".md")]
        refuse = overexposed(everything, idx, matcher, pages)
        if refuse:
            print("refused as phrases rather than names (>%d pages say them):"
                  % PHRASE_PAGE_CAP)
            for n in sorted(refuse):
                print("  %r" % n)
            print()
        n_pages = n_links = 0
        for slug in live:
            txt = pages[slug + ".md"]
            new, applied = apply_unlinked(slug, txt, idx, matcher, refuse)
            if not applied:
                continue
            with open(slug + ".md", "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
                fh.write(new)
            n_pages += 1
            n_links += len(applied)
            print(f"{slug}  +{len(applied)}")
            for name, tgt in applied:
                print(f"      {name!r} -> {tgt}")
        print(f"\n{n_links} link(s) written across {n_pages} page(s)")
        return 0

    total_pages = total_names = 0
    rows = []
    for slug in targets:
        txt = pages[slug + ".md"]
        if under_moratorium(txt, slug + ".md"):
            continue
        fm, _ = split_fm(txt)
        hits = unlinked_names(slug, fm, txt, idx, matcher)
        hits = {k: v for k, v in hits.items()
                if show_low or v["conf"] == "high"}
        if not hits:
            continue
        rows.append((len(hits), slug, hits))
        total_pages += 1
        total_names += len(hits)

    rows.sort(reverse=True)
    single = len(args) == 1
    for n, slug, hits in (rows if single else rows[:40]):
        size = len(pages[slug + ".md"].encode()) // 1024
        have = len(WIKILINK_RE.findall(pages[slug + ".md"]))
        print(f"\n{slug}  ({size}KB, {have} link(s) now, {n} unlinked)")
        for (name, owners), meta in sorted(
                hits.items(), key=lambda kv: -kv[1]["n"]):
            flag = "  ⚠common" if name in COMMON_PHRASE else ""
            amb = f"  [{len(owners)} pages]" if len(owners) > 1 else ""
            print(f"  {meta['n']:>3}x {meta['conf']:<4} {name!r:<28} "
                  f"→ {owners[0]}{amb}{flag}")
            if single:
                ln, line = meta["first"]
                print(f"        L{ln}: {line[:150]}")
    if not single and len(rows) > 40:
        print(f"\n… and {len(rows) - 40} more pages")
    print(f"\n{total_names} unlinked name(s) across {total_pages} page(s)"
          f"{'' if show_low else '  (high confidence only; --low for the rest)'}")
    return 0


# --------------------------------------------------------------- breadcrumb

FOOTER_RE = re.compile(r"\n*\n---\n\n\*\*Up:\*\*[^\n]*\n*\Z")
YEARISH_RE = re.compile(r"^(\d{4})$")


def ancestry(slug, pages):
    """The chain of index pages above a page, nearest last.

    An "index" here is whichever of `<dir>/index` or `<dir>` exists as a page —
    the corpus uses both shapes (`wiki/people/index.md` and `wiki/self/twitter.md`
    are each the parent of their directory) and a reader does not care which.
    """
    out, d = [], os.path.dirname(slug)
    while d and d != "wiki":
        for cand in (d + "/index", d):
            if cand in pages and cand != slug:
                out.append(cand)
                break
        d = os.path.dirname(d)
    return list(reversed(out))


def crumb_label(idx_slug):
    """The section's name, from its directory rather than its own title.

    Most index pages here are titled `index`, and the few that are not are
    titled for the page (`Twitter / X Activity (@danfrank)`) rather than for the
    slot. A breadcrumb wants the slot.
    """
    d = os.path.dirname(idx_slug) if idx_slug.endswith("/index") else idx_slug
    return os.path.basename(d).replace("-", " ").title()


def sequence_neighbours(slug, pages):
    """Previous and next, only where the siblings are a numeric run.

    Deliberately narrow: `wiki/self/twitter/2013` sits in a sequence a reader
    walks, `wiki/people/jerel-coles` does not. Alphabetical order is not a
    sequence and pretending it is invents a reading path nobody chose.
    """
    base = os.path.basename(slug)
    if not YEARISH_RE.match(base):
        return None, None
    d = os.path.dirname(slug)
    sibs = sorted(int(os.path.basename(s)) for s in pages
                  if os.path.dirname(s) == d and YEARISH_RE.match(os.path.basename(s)))
    n = int(base)
    i = sibs.index(n)
    prev = "%s/%d" % (d, sibs[i - 1]) if i > 0 else None
    nxt = "%s/%d" % (d, sibs[i + 1]) if i + 1 < len(sibs) else None
    return prev, nxt


def footer_for(slug, pages):
    chain = ancestry(slug, pages)
    if not chain:
        return None
    parts = ["**Up:** " + " › ".join(
        "[[%s|%s]]" % (c, crumb_label(c)) for c in chain)]
    prev, nxt = sequence_neighbours(slug, pages)
    if prev:
        parts.append("**Previous:** [[%s|%s]]" % (prev, os.path.basename(prev)))
    if nxt:
        parts.append("**Next:** [[%s|%s]]" % (nxt, os.path.basename(nxt)))
    return "\n\n---\n\n" + " · ".join(parts) + "\n"


def needs_breadcrumb(slug, txt, pages, slugs):
    """A page a parent index links to, that offers the reader no way back.

    `STYLE_GUIDE.md` requires every non-index page to be **reachable from** its
    domain index, and `bin/wiki-lint`'s orphan check enforces exactly that.
    Nothing states or checks the other direction, and the corpus went the way
    the rule was written: measured 2026-09-05, **453 of 497 pages never link
    their own index** — every one of the 168 people pages among them. A reader
    arriving from search, or from the portal's front page, lands and stops.

    The test is "does this page link the nearest index above it", not "does that
    index link back to this page". The second is narrower by 53 pages and
    narrower in the wrong direction: a page its own index does not list is more
    stranded than one it does, not less.
    """
    if os.path.basename(slug) == "index" or is_generated_slug(slug):
        return False
    _fm, body = split_fm(txt)
    if FOOTER_RE.search(body):
        return False
    chain = ancestry(slug, slugs)
    if not chain:
        return False
    return chain[-1] not in set(WIKILINK_RE.findall(body))


def cmd_breadcrumb(argv):
    """Give every page linked from an index a way back to it."""
    do_apply = "--apply" in argv
    named = [a[:-3] if a.endswith(".md") else a
             for a in argv if not a.startswith("-")]
    pages = load_pages()
    slugs = {p[:-3] for p in pages}
    targets = named or sorted(slugs)

    n = 0
    for slug in targets:
        if slug + ".md" not in pages:
            print("no such page: %s" % slug, file=sys.stderr)
            return 1
        txt = pages[slug + ".md"]
        if not needs_breadcrumb(slug, txt, pages, slugs):
            continue
        n += 1
        foot = footer_for(slug, slugs)
        if not do_apply:
            print("%-58s %s" % (slug, foot.strip().replace("\n", " ")))
            continue
        fm, body = split_fm(txt)
        head = txt[:len(txt) - len(body)]
        with open(slug + ".md", "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            fh.write(head + body.rstrip("\n") + foot)
    print("\n%d page(s) %s" % (n, "given a way back" if do_apply
                               else "linked from an index with no way back"))
    return 0


# --------------------------------------------------------------- rederive

NOT_REDERIVED = re.compile(r"has not been re-derived", re.I)
REDERIVED = re.compile(r"\bRE-DERIVED\b")
# Reuses `counts`' MSG_COUNT_RE rather than defining a second one. The first cut
# of this file declared its own at module scope and **silently shadowed that
# one** — a stricter pattern requiring `**bold**`, which quietly changed what
# `counts` matched. `bin/wiki-check` reported all gates clean; only
# `python3 -m unittest discover -s tests` saw it, which is the reason CLAUDE.md
# lists the two separately.


def cmd_rederive(argv):
    """Pages that admit their own prose predates half their evidence.

    **The debt `counts` created and could not measure.** On 2026-09-04 that
    command found thirteen pages reporting a Received-only figure as a whole
    thread; the fix landed a correction blockquote on each, and the honest ones
    say so in as many words — *"the prose below was written against the smaller,
    one-sided thread and has not been re-derived."* The number was then right
    and the page was still written from half a conversation.

    Thirty-five pages carry that sentence, and until this command existed the
    only way to find them was a grep somebody had to think of running — the
    exact shape `bin/wiki-work` exists to end, and the exact shape that let 161
    unlinked twitter mentions stand through two crosslink passes. Ordered by
    thread size, because the unread half of a 770-message thread is worth more
    than the unread half of a 24-message one.

    A page leaves this list when its correction block is replaced by the
    re-derivation, not when somebody ticks it off. Three have been done
    (`new-jim-shaffer`, `vaughn`, `davey-fitzpatrick`) and each produced a
    page-level finding the count correction alone did not: a first-person
    statement of intent, a drug-test countermeasure narrated to management, and
    a $14,000 transfer that turned out to have a date and a stated purpose.
    """
    pages = load_pages()
    rows = []
    for path, txt in pages.items():
        if not NOT_REDERIVED.search(txt):
            continue
        m = MSG_COUNT_RE.search(txt)
        n = int(m.group(1).replace(",", "")) if m else 0
        rows.append((n, path[:-3], bool(REDERIVED.search(txt))))
    rows.sort(reverse=True)
    print("Pages whose prose was written from half a thread, biggest first.\n"
          "The count is right on every one of them; the reading is not.\n")
    constrained = 0
    for n, slug, part in rows:
        # **Constrained, not refused.** The standing directive forbids new
        # narrative about one living person; it does not forbid re-deriving a
        # page that mentions her. But on a page whose unread half is mostly
        # about her, most of what a re-derivation would find is material that
        # cannot be written — `wiki/people/danny-matthews` is 770 messages
        # whose dominant subject is the arrangement she is half of. Flagged so
        # the next session learns that before spending the hour, and counted
        # rather than dropped, because a page nobody may finish and a page
        # nobody has got to must not look the same from outside.
        heavy = len(MORATORIUM.findall(pages[slug + ".md"])) >= 5
        constrained += heavy
        print("  %7s  %s%s%s" % (
            n or "?", slug,
            "   (partly re-derived)" if part else "",
            "   ⚠ directive-constrained" if heavy else ""))
    print("\n%d page(s) owed a re-derivation, %d of them directive-constrained."
          % (len(rows), constrained))
    print("`bin/wiki-crosslink scan <page> --conversation` reads the thread; "
          "the operation is REWRITE in CLAUDE.md.")
    return 0


def main(argv):
    if not argv:
        print(USAGE)
        return 1
    cmd, rest = argv[0], argv[1:]
    fns = {"entities": cmd_entities, "scan": cmd_scan, "reciprocal": cmd_reciprocal,
           "orphaned": cmd_orphaned, "check": cmd_check, "coverage": cmd_coverage,
           "handles": cmd_handles, "counts": cmd_counts,
           "unlinked": cmd_unlinked,
           "breadcrumb": cmd_breadcrumb,
           "rederive": cmd_rederive}
    if cmd not in fns:
        print(USAGE)
        return 1
    return fns[cmd](rest)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
